1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:02,945
so... you ask an AI assistant which laptop to buy.

2
00:00:02,995 --> 00:00:04,204
It names one.

3
00:00:04,254 --> 00:00:07,159
Now — how did *that* laptop get in there?

4
00:00:07,209 --> 00:00:08,617
There are <font color="#00FFFF">four placements</font>.

5
00:00:08,667 --> 00:00:11,193
And the <font color="#FFD700">sponsored label</font> is on exactly one.

6
00:00:11,243 --> 00:00:13,429
On Google, the laptop ad is a <font color="#4ADE80">slot</font>.

7
00:00:13,479 --> 00:00:16,644
Top of the page, with a <font color="#FFD700">visual label</font> on it.

8
00:00:16,694 --> 00:00:19,081
Half of people still miss the label.

9
00:00:19,131 --> 00:00:20,798
An AI response has none.

10
00:00:20,848 --> 00:00:25,313
But the slot is a <font color="#4ADE80">thing</font>. It has an edge.

11
00:00:25,363 --> 00:00:27,690
Move it. Remove it. Don't click it.

12
00:00:27,790 --> 00:00:29,790
<font color="#FFD700">RULE ONE: a label works on a slot. Not on a sentence.</font>

13
00:00:29,740 --> 00:00:33,205
In a chat there are <font color="#00FFFF">four placements</font> for that laptop.

14
00:00:33,255 --> 00:00:37,859
A <font color="#4ADE80">slot under the answer</font> — the model never sees it.

15
00:00:37,909 --> 00:00:41,495
<font color="#00FFFF">The list</font> — the laptop sits inside the recommendations.

16
00:00:41,545 --> 00:00:43,313
<font color="#FF5252">The laptop's page</font>, retrieved.

17
00:00:43,363 --> 00:00:46,788
And the <font color="#FF5252">system prompt</font> — the operator's words, not yours.

18
00:00:46,838 --> 00:00:49,303
Every placement <font color="#4ADE80">except the separate slot</font> <font color="#FF5252">changes the answer</font>.

19
00:00:49,353 --> 00:00:53,916
One line in the system prompt — <font color="#FF5252">eighteen of twenty-three</font> models picked the sponsored laptop.

20
00:00:53,966 --> 00:00:55,034
At <font color="#FF5252">twice the price</font>.

21
00:00:55,084 --> 00:00:57,511
Part 11 called this <font color="#FF5252">prompt injection</font>.

22
00:00:57,561 --> 00:01:00,585
Same mechanism — this time the operator is the one injecting.

23
00:01:00,635 --> 00:01:01,424
And the label?

24
00:01:01,474 --> 00:01:04,500
Labelled, the sponsored pick still won <font color="#FF5252">fifty-five</font> percent.

25
00:01:04,550 --> 00:01:05,379
Unlabelled, <font color="#FF5252">sixty-one</font>.

26
00:01:05,429 --> 00:01:06,817
On Google — <font color="#4ADE80">twenty-two</font>.

27
00:01:06,867 --> 00:01:09,752
<font color="#FFD700">RULE TWO: past the slot, the label stops working.</font>

28
00:01:09,802 --> 00:01:13,665
Can you tell? <font color="#FF5252">Under ten percent</font>, when the model is told to be subtle.

29
00:01:13,715 --> 00:01:16,460
Half of people miss an ad even when it's disclosed.

30
00:01:16,510 --> 00:01:20,594
And no regulator has a rule for an ad that isn't a thing.

31
00:01:20,644 --> 00:01:23,330
One check works. Same question, <font color="#4ADE80">sponsor removed</font>.

32
00:01:23,380 --> 00:01:25,208
Different laptop? That's the placement.

33
00:01:25,258 --> 00:01:30,380
Building an assistant? That <font color="#4ADE80">diff</font> is a list and a replay, written before the slot exists.

34
00:01:30,430 --> 00:01:33,215
Using one? <font color="#4ADE80">Ask twice</font> — two assistants, same question.

35
00:01:33,265 --> 00:01:39,170
Not proof. But it's the only check on your side.

36
00:01:39,220 --> 00:01:44,562
<font color="#FFD700">RULE THREE: the only check is a diff. Build it before the slot — or ask twice.</font>

37
00:01:44,612 --> 00:01:48,197
<font color="#FFD700">Product track, part one — it stands on Part 11.</font>

38
00:01:48,247 --> 00:01:51,472
<font color="#FFD700">The one-page list is in the vault, link in bio.</font>

39
00:01:51,522 --> 00:01:52,590
<font color="#FFD700">Follow for both tracks.</font>
